      <section class="mb-12">
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Walk into any well-run kindergarten in Bangsar or Petaling Jaya during a craft week and you'll see the same thing — heaps of corrugated cardboard being turned into shop counters, rocket ships, dinosaur caves, and pretend ATMs. Cardboard is the perfect children's material: cheap, safe, structural, paintable, and recyclable when the project ends. Quinocycle supplies corrugated sheets to schools, kindergartens, and parents across Klang Valley who want to spend an afternoon (or a term) building something real. Here's how to spec, source, and make the most of corrugated cardboard for kids.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">Cardboard Playhouses & Forts at Home</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Living-Room Playhouse Builds</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A child-height playhouse needs four wall panels (each ~1m wide x 1.2m tall), a roof, a door cut-out, and one or two windows. Five-ply double-wall holds its shape against energetic four-year-olds. Use packing tape on the inside corners and let the kids paint the exterior with poster paints — the kraft surface takes paint beautifully.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Modular Fort Building</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">For sustained imaginative play, cut interlocking notches into smaller panels (450x600mm) so kids can rebuild different fort shapes. This is an entire afternoon's activity in itself, and the storage footprint is just a flat stack under the bed.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Outdoor Use & Weather Considerations</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Corrugated cardboard is not weatherproof. For shaded balcony or covered-porch use it survives months. Direct sun and rain will collapse it within days. Bring it inside between play sessions for any playhouse you want to last.</p>
      </section>

