        <h2 class="font-headline text-2xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-6">School & Kindergarten STEM Kits</h2>
        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Cardboard Engineering Modules</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">Cardboard is the easiest way to introduce structural engineering to primary kids. A class of 20 building bridges from corrugated sheets learns about beams, trusses, and load-spreading by trial and error. Pre-cut "engineering panels" (200x300mm) speed setup; let the kids decide how to join them with glue dots or tape.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Maker-Space & Robotics Chassis</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">After-school robotics clubs use corrugated cardboard to prototype robot chassis before committing to acrylic or 3D-printed parts. It's faster, cheaper, and safer for kids to test mechanical ideas in cardboard first. Many schools standardise on 5-ply for its stiffness-to-weight ratio.</p>

        <h3 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-on-surface mb-4">Marble Run & Physics Demonstrations</h3>
        <p class="font-body text-on-surface leading-relaxed mb-6">A wall-mounted marble-run made from cardboard channels demonstrates gravity, momentum, and angle of incidence. The whole rig comes off and gets stored flat. Schools across Selangor have built marble-run installations as semester showpieces.</p>
      </section>

